Make a dispatch miss say how execution got there, in the file a crash keeps.

A miss halts the CPU (nds_halt), so for a played session - a double-clicked
exe whose stderr goes nowhere - dispatch_misses.log is the only record. It held
pc and lr only. One crash left "pc=0x0005D630 lr=0x0000104C":
ITCM code (the collision routine at 0x01FF8ABC, whose `bl 0x02055630` sits at
0x01FF9048) executed through the ITCM's low mirror, so its relative branch
resolved to 0x0005D630. Nothing in the log could say what jumped into the
mirror.

So the miss now also records the time, the guest registers and the runtime's
own stack of pending guest returns (runtime_call_stack_data, already used by
patch 0007's unmapped-access report) - in the file, and on stderr for a drive.

--- a/runner/src/runtime_arm.cpp
+++ b/runner/src/runtime_arm.cpp
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 #include <cstdio>
 #include <cstdlib>
 #include <cstring>
+#include <ctime>
 #include <vector>
 
 #include "state.h"
@@ -1172,6 +1173,15 @@
     std::fprintf(stderr,
         "[dispatch-miss] cpu=%s pc=0x%08X %s (lr=0x%08X)\n",
         cpu, t, mode, g_cpu.R[14]);
+    {
+        const uint32_t depth = runtime_call_stack_depth();
+        const uint32_t* ret = runtime_call_stack_data();
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "[dispatch-miss]   pending guest returns "
+                     "(innermost first, depth %u):", unsigned(depth));
+        for (uint32_t i = 0; i < depth && i < 24u; ++i)
+            std::fprintf(stderr, " %08X", unsigned(ret[depth - 1u - i]));
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "\n");
+    }
 
     // A miss INSIDE recompiled static ROM is a real non-function-start entry
